Find the second derivative of the function $f(x) = \cos(\log(2-4x)) - \sqrt{4x+9}$.
To find the second derivative of $f(x)$, let's first find the first derivative and then differentiate it again.

First derivative:
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= -\frac{d}{dx}[\cos(\log(2-4x))] - \frac{d}{dx}[\sqrt{4x+9}] \\
&= -\sin(\log(2-4x)) \cdot \frac{d}{dx}[\log(2-4x)] - \frac{1}{2}(4x+9)^{-1/2} \cdot \frac{d}{dx}[4x+9] \\
&= -\sin(\log(2-4x)) \cdot \frac{-4}{2-4x} - \frac{1}{2}(4x+9)^{-1/2} \cdot 4 \\
&= \frac{4\sin(\log(2-4x))}{4x-2} - \frac{2}{\sqrt{4x+9}}.
\end{align*}

Second derivative:
\begin{align*}
f''(x) &= -\frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{4\sin(\log(2-4x))}{4x-2}\right] + \frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{2}{\sqrt{4x+9}}\right] \\
&= -\left[4\frac{(4x-2)\cos(\log(2-4x))\cdot(-4)}{(4x-2)^2} - \frac{4\sin(\log(2-4x))}{(4x-2)^2}\right] - \frac{2}{2}(4x+9)^{-3/2} \cdot 4 \\
&= \frac{16\cos(\log(2-4x))}{(4x-2)^2} - \frac{4\sin(\log(2-4x))}{(4x-2)^2} - \frac{4}{(4x+9)^{3/2}} \\
&= \frac{4\cos(\log(2-4x))}{(2-4x)^2} - \frac{\sin(\log(2-4x))}{(2-4x)^2} - \frac{4}{(4x+9)^{3/2}}.
